---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

---
<BaseLayout title="BIMI: Display Your Logo in Inboxes" description="BIMI explained: the DNS record that puts your verified logo next to your email, VMC requirements, and provider support."  
 breadcrumb={[["Home", "/"], ["Email Authentication", "/email-authentication/"], ["BIMI"]]}>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-12">
    <nav class="mb-4 text-sm text-ink-faint" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-accent">Home</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <a href="/email-authentication/" class="hover:text-accent">Email Authentication</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span class="text-ink-dim">BIMI</span>
    </nav>
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl">BIMI: Your Logo in the Inbox</h1>
    <div class="pd-answer" data-vs="answer">
      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">
      BIMI (Brand Indicators for Message Identification) lets participating mailbox providers show
      your verified logo beside your authenticated email. It is the visible reward for having your
      invisible authentication in order.
    </p>
    </div>
<nav class="pd-toc" aria-label="On this page">
      <p class="font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-accent">On this page</p>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="#prerequisites-strictly-enforced">Prerequisites (strictly enforced)</a></li>
        <li><a href="#the-record">The record</a></li>
        <li><a href="#who-shows-it">Who shows it</a></li>
        <li><a href="#is-it-worth-it">Is it worth it?</a></li>
        <li><a href="#related">Related</a></li>
      </ul>
    </nav>
    <div class="rich-text mt-8">
      <h2 id="prerequisites-strictly-enforced">Prerequisites (strictly enforced)</h2>
      <ol class="list-decimal space-y-1.5 pl-5">
        <li>SPF and DKIM passing with alignment.</li>
        <li><a href="/dmarc/">DMARC</a> at enforcement: p=quarantine or p=reject at minimum for major providers.</li>
        <li>A BIMI TXT record at <code>default._domainkey</code>-style location: <code>yourdomain._bimi</code>, carrying <code>v=BIMI1; l=...</code> pointing to your SVG logo.</li>
        <li>A Verified Mark Certificate (VMC) for the big providers: a trademark-verified certificate proving you own the logo, purchased from a CA (DigiCert, Entrust).</li>
      </ol>
      <h2 id="the-record">The record</h2>
      <pre class="rounded-lg border border-line bg-panel-2 p-4 font-mono text-xs leading-relaxed text-accent-soft">yourdomain.com.  IN  TXT  "v=BIMI1; l=https://yourdomain.com/logo.svg; a=https://yourdomain.com/vmc.pem"</pre>
      <p>
        The logo must be a tiny SVG in the BIMI profile spec; the <code>a=</code> tag carries the VMC.
        Providers cache aggressively, so changes take time to appear.
      </p>
      <h2 id="who-shows-it">Who shows it</h2>
      <p>
        Support is strongest in Apple Mail (iOS/macOS), Gmail (with VMC), Yahoo, and Fastmail.
        Microsoft's rollout has been partial. Where supported, the effect is real: brand recognition
        in the inbox row, and spoofers without your DNS cannot imitate it.
      </p>
      <h2 id="is-it-worth-it">Is it worth it?</h2>
      <p>
        If you send significant volume to consumer inboxes and already run DMARC at enforcement, the
        marginal cost is one SVG and a VMC fee. If you are still at p=none, BIMI is a later stop on
        the same road: use the <a href="/dmarc-record-checker/">DMARC checker</a> to see where you stand.
      </p>
      
<h2 id="related">Related</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/dmarc/">DMARC explained</a></li>
        <li><a href="/mta-sts/">MTA-STS</a>, the other advanced email-auth extension</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
